# LICENSE file in the root directory of this source tree.
#
# REFUSE a merge when a review refusal is bound to the exact head.
#
# ⚠️ WHY THIS EXISTS: THERE WAS NO GATE. Measured 2026-08-25, `grep -rn
# CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT` across every .py/.sh/.rs in this repository returned hits
# in ci-hub/health/approval_binding.py and IN ITS OWN TESTS, and nowhere else. No
# lander, no merge gate, no workflow read a refusal marker. approval_binding.py is
# reached only by ci-hub/health/pr_status.py, which REPORTS. The merge itself is
# `gh pr merge` run by an agent, so the only thing between a live refusal and a
# landing was whether that agent happened to read the comments.
#
# hermit#2591 is what that costs:
#     17:55:19Z  APPROVED-AT: claude 86b76b82           (column 0)
#     18:05:19Z  CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude 86b76b82  (COLUMN 0, plain, parses)
#     18:09:01Z  MERGED
#     18:12:00Z  passed-review-claude labeled -- three minutes AFTER the merge
# A plain, parseable, binding refusal was live 3m42s before the merge, and there
# was no approval label at merge time either. Neither the marker path nor the
# label path was consulted, because neither was wired to anything.
#
# ⚠️ A CONVENTION ENFORCED BY EVERY PARTICIPANT REMEMBERING IS NOT A GATE. It fails
# the first time one participant does not. Five separate fixes on 2026-08-25 --
# the parser fail-open, the lander refusal, the loud-on-unknown net, the fourth
# variant, the stale label -- all made readers better at NOTICING a block. None of
# them refuses. This one refuses.
#
# ⚠️ IT KEYS ON A MARKER ASSERTION, NOT TOKEN PRESENCE. After the shared
# Markdown normalization, the marker starts the line or follows an existing lane
# heading or ``VERDICT:`` prefix. Marker text inside arbitrary prose is a mention.
# The marker may be malformed and still fail closed: a full SHA in prose does not
# make it a verdict, and a missing SHA on a standalone marker does not disappear.
#
# If a sha can be extracted and it names the head, REFUSE. If it names another
# commit, the objection was outrun but remains unresolved until later canonical
# current-head evidence answers it. AND IF NO SHA CAN BE EXTRACTED AT ALL, REFUSE:
# a marker whose binding cannot be determined must not be waved through, because
# "I could not read it" is not "it does not apply". That is the case the fourth
# variant occupied.
#
# ⚠️ AND IT REFUSES WHEN IT CANNOT RUN. Every path that fails to produce an answer
# -- unresolvable head, unreadable comment container, unreachable API -- returns
# INDETERMINATE (2), and both callers treat non-zero as blocking. A gate that can
# skip itself when its environment is wrong reproduces the defect it fixes: the
# existing admission check was found silently DISABLED by a stale primary, which
# is the same shape as a marker that cannot be bound being read as absent.
#
# ⚠️ THE VERDICT IS A LINE ON STDOUT. SILENCE IS NOT CLEARANCE.
#
# Until 2026-08-26 only CLEAR was printed to stdout and every other verdict went
# to stderr, so `gate ... | tail` -- the way it is actually run -- showed a line
# for the one outcome that permits a merge and NOTHING for the three that forbid
# one. Combined with the shell's own behaviour that made an empty stdout mean four
# different things, three of which forbid merging:
#
#     gate present, CLEAR         stdout: one CLEAR line     rc 0
#     gate present, BLOCKED       stdout: EMPTY              rc 1, eaten by the pipe
#     gate present, MOOT/INDET    stdout: EMPTY              rc 3/2, eaten by the pipe
#     ⚠ GATE FILE ABSENT          stdout: EMPTY              rc 0 -- tail's status
#
# The last row is the census result of 2026-08-26: 49 of 76 dev-hermit checkouts on
# devbig014 -- including the shared primary at /home/newton/work/dev-hermit, 334
# commits behind and the tree most agents reach for first -- do not contain this
# file at all. Running it there and piping the result reports SUCCESS, and reads
# as "no refusal, proceed". That is the CLAUDE.md `$?`-after-a-pipe trap landing
# on the tool that decides whether a head may merge.
#
# THE RULE IS NOW THE OTHER WAY ROUND. Every terminal path prints exactly one
#
#     changes-requested-gate: <VERDICT> pr=... head=... gate=<sha12|UNKNOWN> -- ...
#
# line to STDOUT, including the crash handler. So a reader gets a positive
# statement in all four cases, and an EMPTY stdout no longer means "clear" -- it
# means the gate did not run, which is the one thing the old shape could not say.
# `no_verdict_in()` is the caller-side spelling of that, and land-pr.sh uses it:
# rc 0 with no verdict line is refused rather than trusted.
#
# ⚠️ AND EVERY VERDICT NAMES THE COPY THAT ISSUED IT. `gate=` is the ci-hub commit
# this file was read from. A stale gate does not announce that it is stale -- it
# emits a confident verdict in the current format, and on an OPEN pull request an
# old copy and a new one are indistinguishable. They diverge exactly where it
# matters: measured 2026-08-26 on six pull requests, a copy predating 8d076b4c
# said `rc=0 CLEAR` on three MERGED ones and `rc=1 BLOCKED` on three CLOSED ones
# with the advice "this clears when the AUTHOR pushes a fix", to branches that no
# longer exist. Both answers are wrong the same way and only one looks wrong. Two
# agents can run "the gate" on one pull request in the same minute and be told
# opposite things; `gate=` is what lets a reader tell which one was old.
#
# Usage:
#   changes-requested-gate --repo OWNER/REPO --pr N [--head SHA]
#
# Exit status:
#   0  no refusal binds this head -- the merge may proceed
#   1  BLOCKED: a refusal is bound to this exact head
#   2  INDETERMINATE: a marker exists that cannot be bound, or the data could not
#      be fetched. Callers MUST treat this as blocking; it is not "no".
#   3  MOOT: the pull request is not OPEN, so the question no longer applies.
#
#   ⚠ NO exit status means "the gate was not here". Absence is reported by the
#   ABSENCE OF A VERDICT LINE, never by a status -- a status cannot survive a
#   pipe and a missing file cannot choose one.
# `from __future__ import annotations` so the PEP 604 / PEP 585 annotations below
# do not evaluate at import time. Without it this file raises TypeError on an
# older python3 -- measured: /usr/bin/python3 crashed at the first annotated def.
# A gate that dies on import still fails CLOSED here, because both callers treat
# non-zero as blocking, but it reported BLOCKED for what was really COULD-NOT-RUN,
# and recording "I could not check" as "a reviewer refused" is a false verdict.
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

# ⚠️ THE FENCE RULE IS IMPORTED, NOT COPIED. This gate already carries its own
# marker grammar, and that second copy is exactly what diverged from
# gh-merge-verified: measured 2026-08-25, the two paths disagreed on 6 of 28 open
# heads. Eligibility -- WHICH LINES may carry a verdict at all -- now has one
# definition in ci-hub/health/approval_binding.py and both landers call it.
# Policy still differs here (this gate fails closed on an unbindable refusal); the
# grammar and the eligibility rule may not.
def _load_grammar():
    import importlib.util
    import pathlib
    target = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "health" / "approval_binding.py"
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("approval_binding", target)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(module)
    return module

OK, BLOCKED, INDETERMINATE = 0, 1, 2
# ⚠ A FOURTH OUTCOME, BECAUSE "MAY THIS MERGE?" HAS A FOURTH ANSWER: the
# question no longer applies. Non-zero on purpose -- land-pr.sh dispatches
# `0) clear ;; *) abandon ;;`, so MOOT abandons exactly like BLOCKED and no caller
# can mistake it for permission. It is DISTINCT from BLOCKED because BLOCKED names
# a reviewer who refused and prescribes a remedy; on a merged pull request there is
# no reviewer to satisfy and no branch left to push to.
MOOT = 3

LABELS = {OK: "CLEAR", BLOCKED: "BLOCKED", INDETERMINATE: "INDETERMINATE", MOOT: "MOOT"}

VERDICT_PREFIX = "changes-requested-gate:"

# ⚠️ THE DETECTOR CANNOT BE THE TOOL'S NAME, BECAUSE ABSENCE SAYS THE TOOL'S NAME.
# The first version of this keyed on the bare prefix, which is the string already
# at the front of every line this tool has ever printed -- and the self-test
# immediately rejected it, because the diagnostic the caller gets INSTEAD of a
# verdict is
#
#     bash: ./ci-hub/bin/changes-requested-gate: No such file or directory
#     python3: can't open file '.../ci-hub/bin/changes-requested-gate': [Errno 2] ...
#
# and both contain `changes-requested-gate:`. land-pr.sh captures the gate with
# `2>&1`, so a prefix-only detector would have read the operating system's report
# that THE GATE IS MISSING as proof that the gate had spoken -- the same defect
# one layer further out, and this time announcing itself in the very text it
# misreads. Same family as the near-miss net that had to be stopped from firing on
# this file's own name.
#
# A verdict is therefore the prefix at the START of a line followed by one of the
# four labels. The tool controls both; a path in a diagnostic satisfies neither.
_VERDICT_LINE = re.compile(
    r"^changes-requested-gate: (?:CLEAR|BLOCKED|INDETERMINATE|MOOT)\b", re.MULTILINE
)


def no_verdict_in(output: str) -> bool:
    """Did this output contain no verdict, i.e. did the gate not run?

    ⚠️ THE CALLER-SIDE HALF OF THE FIX, AND IT IS THE HALF THAT SURVIVES ABSENCE.
    Everything else in this file can only help a reader once this file is on
    disk. When it is not -- 49 of 76 checkouts on 2026-08-26 -- there is nothing
    here to print anything, and the shell reports `No such file or directory` on
    stderr and, through a pipe, status 0. A caller that keys on the status alone
    therefore reads a MISSING GATE as permission.

    So the authorising condition is two facts, not one: status 0 AND a verdict
    line. A gate that did not run cannot produce the second, which is exactly
    what makes absence expressible. Callers must not relax this into
    `rc == 0`.
    """
    return _VERDICT_LINE.search(output or "") is None


def gate_provenance() -> str:
    """The ci-hub commit this file was read from -- 12 hex, or a stated reason.

    ⚠️ A VERDICT THAT CANNOT NAME ITS SOURCE MUST BE TRUSTED; ONE THAT CAN, CAN BE
    CHECKED. This is the fix for the other half of the census: 20 of 76 checkouts
    carry an OLD copy of this gate, and an old copy is indistinguishable from a
    current one on any OPEN pull request. Stamping the commit costs one `git`
    invocation per run and makes "which gate said that" answerable afterwards,
    from the transcript, without re-running anything.

    Never raises and never blocks a verdict. A gate that refused to answer
    because it could not describe itself would be a worse failure than the one
    this repairs, so every error path returns a word instead of a sha.
    """
    here = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-C", str(here), "log", "-1", "--format=%h", "--abbrev=12", "--", str(__file__)],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return "UNKNOWN"
    sha = r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else ""
    if not sha:
        return "UNKNOWN"
    # ⚠ A DIRTY COPY IS NOT THE COMMIT IT SITS ON. Uncommitted edits to this file
    # are how a verdict gets attributed to a landed fix that is not the code that
    # ran. Say so in the stamp rather than printing a sha that is a near-truth.
    try:
        d = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-C", str(here), "status", "--porcelain", "--", str(__file__)],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10,
        )
        if d.returncode == 0 and d.stdout.strip():
            return f"{sha}-dirty"
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass
    return sha


def emit(rc: int, pr: object, head: object, why: str) -> int:
    """Print the one verdict line, on STDOUT, and return the status.

    Every terminal path goes through here. That is the invariant the self-test
    checks: a path that prints its own verdict to stderr reintroduces the empty
    stdout that a reader cannot tell apart from a gate that was never there.
    """
    shown = str(head or "????????")[:12]
    print(
        f"{VERDICT_PREFIX} {LABELS.get(rc, 'INDETERMINATE')} pr={pr} head={shown} "
        f"gate={gate_provenance()} -- {why}"
    )
    # Unbuffered enough to survive a caller that dies right after reading us.
    sys.stdout.flush()
    return rc


REFUSAL = "CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT"
WITHDRAWN = "CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN-AT"
# ⚠️ THE SUFFIX-LESS SPELLING, WHICH FAILED OPEN. A reviewer who writes
# `CHANGES-REQUESTED: claude <sha>` -- bare line, column zero, correct lane,
# correct 40-hex, one token wrong -- was flagged malformed and NOT HONOURED: this
# gate returned CLEAR and the lane's approval stood. hermit#1689 lost two blocking
# reviews that way and sat 19 days; hermit#2611 merged 24 minutes after a block
# written in this form.
#
# THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT. A malformed REFUSAL must still refuse and a
# malformed APPROVAL must not bind, because the two errors are not symmetric:
# someone intending to block is not a weaker signal than someone intending
# nothing. Reading an unparseable refusal as "proceed" inverts the author's
# stated intent; reading it as "stop" costs a re-post.
# ⚠️ THE SUFFIX-LESS WITHDRAWAL, WHICH CREATED A BLOCK INSTEAD OF CLEARING ONE.
# `CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN <sha>` -- no `-AT` -- misses WITHDRAWN, misses
# REFUSAL, and hits REFUSAL_BARE, so a botched attempt to DISCHARGE an objection
# was recorded as a fresh objection. Measured 2026-08-26: that line alone
# returned BLOCKED, and posted alongside a real refusal it produced "2 refusal(s)
# ... from lane(s) claude,unlaned". An agent following the remediation text and
# mistyping it made the head MORE blocked.
#
# This is the exact asymmetry REFUSAL_BARE already exists for, in the other
# direction: the suffix-less spelling of a token must get the same treatment as
# the canonical one. It maps to WITHDRAWN, listed before the refusal tokens so
# longest-first still resolves correctly.
#
# ⚠️ IT STILL CANNOT CLEAR ANYTHING. A withdrawal needs a readable LANE to retire
# a refusal, and this spelling carries none, so it is inert in both directions --
# it neither blocks nor discharges. That is the only safe polarity: an unreadable
# withdrawal must not wave a refusal through, and must not manufacture one.
WITHDRAWN_BARE = "CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN"
REFUSAL_BARE = "CHANGES-REQUESTED"

_SHA = re.compile(r"\b([0-9a-f]{7,40})\b")
_LANE = re.compile(r"\b(claude|codex)\b", re.I)

# ⚠️ A WITHDRAWAL NAMES THE REFUSAL IT RETIRES, BY COMMENT ID.
#
# Ruled 2026-08-25 after a withdrawal cleared another agent's live refusal on
# hermit#2625. Two same-lane refusals are INDEPENDENT; retiring one must not
# retire the other.
#
# BY COMMENT ID AND NOT BY ISSUER, and hermit#2625 is why. The obvious cheaper
# scheme -- read the issuer out of the `[hermit2, <agent>, ...]` disclosure tag --
# works on invented input and FAILS ON THE REAL PAIR: of the two refusals at
# f013ac4b, hermit-012's carries a tag and hermit-015's carries NONE. The refusal
# that must keep binding is exactly the one with no readable issuer. A comment id
# is assigned by GitHub and is always present, so it is the only identity that
# cannot go missing.
#
# Two refusals from different agents also have BYTE-IDENTICAL marker text
# (`CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude <sha>`), so "quote the exact text" cannot
# separate them either.
# ⚠️ A DOCUMENTATION EXAMPLE IS NOT A REFUSAL, AND THIS COST A REAL LANDING.
# Measured 2026-08-25 on hermit#2611: a comment explaining the convention contained
#     CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude <sha>   ->  refuses, clears the lane
# which is line-initial, names a lane, and carries no hex -- so the gate returned
# INDETERMINATE and refused a head that had just been approved. That is precisely
# the "blocks a pull request for EXPLAINING the rule" failure this gate's own
# docstring warns gets it switched off, and it appeared in a comment posted AFTER
# the corpus measurement that found zero false positives.
#
# The discriminator is narrow on purpose: an ANGLE-BRACKET PLACEHOLDER where the
# sha belongs. A reviewer refusing a head writes a sha, or writes prose -- they do
# not write `<sha>`. So `CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude -- see above` still refuses
# (no sha, no placeholder, genuinely unbindable), and only the template form is
# treated as documentation.
# The placeholder rule now lives in the shared grammar; see is_template there.
# Kept as a name so the docstring above still reads, but sourced from one place.

def gh_json(args: list[str]) -> object:
    # with-proxy is what makes gh reach the network on these hosts; without it the
    # call fails as "network is unreachable" and would otherwise be indistinguishable
    # from "no comments", which is exactly the fail-open this gate exists to remove.
    for argv in (["with-proxy", "gh", *args], ["gh", *args]):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=180)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            continue
        if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip():
            try:
                return json.loads(r.stdout)
            except json.JSONDecodeError:
                continue
    return None


def comment_bodies_and_ids(records: object) -> tuple[list[str], list[object] | None]:
    """Return comment bodies with the GitHub ids that identify their records.

    A legacy ``--from-json`` array of body strings has no stable ids, version
    times, review surfaces, or immutable GitHub actors. It is recognized here so
    the command can refuse it explicitly. GitHub's three review endpoints return
    objects; those must carry an id. Silently replacing a missing id with the
    record's position would let ``RETIRES 0`` name whichever comment happened to
    be first, so malformed object input is refused instead of assigned an id.
    """
    if not isinstance(records, list):
        raise ValueError("comment input is not a JSON array")
    if not records:
        return [], []
    if all(isinstance(record, str) for record in records):
        return list(records), None
    if not all(isinstance(record, dict) for record in records):
        raise ValueError("comment input mixes body strings with comment objects")

    grammar = _load_grammar()
    bodies: list[str] = []
    ids: list[object] = []
    for position, record in enumerate(records):
        cid = grammar.comment_database_id(record)
        if cid is None:
            raise ValueError(f"comment object {position} has no readable numeric id")
        body = record.get("body")
        if body is None:
            body = ""
        if not isinstance(body, str):
            raise ValueError(f"comment object {position} has a non-string body")
        bodies.append(body)
        ids.append(cid)
    return bodies, ids


def native_changes_requested_ids(records: object) -> set[str]:
    """Numeric ids of native GitHub CHANGES_REQUESTED review records."""
    if not isinstance(records, list):
        return set()
    grammar = _load_grammar()
    return {
        record_id
        for record in records
        if isinstance(record, dict)
        and (record.get("state") or "") == "CHANGES_REQUESTED"
        and (record_id := grammar.comment_database_id(record)) is not None
    }


def live_review_records(repo: str, pr: object) -> tuple[list[dict], str | None]:
    """Read the three GitHub review surfaces with actor and version metadata."""
    grammar = _load_grammar()
    records: list[dict] = []
    for path, surface in (
        (f"repos/{repo}/issues/{pr}/comments?per_page=100", "issue-comment"),
        (
            f"repos/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/comments?per_page=100",
            "pull-review-comment",
        ),
    ):
        pages = gh_json(["api", path, "--paginate", "--slurp"])
        try:
            records.extend(grammar.review_records_from_rest_pages(pages, surface))
        except grammar.ReviewRecordError as error:
            return [], f"{path.split('?')[0]} returned malformed records: {error}"

    parts = repo.split("/")
    if len(parts) != 2 or not all(parts):
        return [], f"repository name {repo!r} is not owner/name"
    owner, name = parts
    pages = gh_json(
        [
            "api",
            "graphql",
            "--paginate",
            "--slurp",
            "-f",
            f"owner={owner}",
            "-f",
            f"name={name}",
            "-F",
            f"number={pr}",
            "-f",
            f"query={grammar.NATIVE_REVIEWS_QUERY}",
        ]
    )
    try:
        records.extend(grammar.native_review_records_from_graphql(pages))
        records = grammar.annotate_current_review_authority(
            records,
            repo,
            lambda path: gh_json(["api", path]),
        )
    except grammar.ReviewRecordError as error:
        return [], str(error)
    return records, None


def markers(bodies: list[str]) -> list[tuple[str, str | None, str | None]]:
    return [(k, l, sh) for k, l, sh, _cid in markers_with_ids(list(enumerate(bodies)))]



APPROVAL = "APPROVED-AT"


def approvals_in_order(items):
    """Canonical approvals in comment order as ``(lane, sha, id, position)``.

    ⚠️ COLLECTED SEPARATELY FROM `markers_with_ids` ON PURPOSE. That list drives
    refusal counting, `binds()`, and the withdrawal collapse logic; an APPROVED
    entry leaking into it would change verdicts nowhere near this feature. This
    reads the same bodies with the same decoration stripping and the same
    citation suppression, and returns its own list.

    ⚠️ POSITION IS COMMENT ORDER, NOT SHA RECENCY. The classification below must
    not infer which sha is newest -- "a marker that adopts whatever sha is newest
    is exactly how an approval outlives the code it approved", as the block above
    says. Chronology is a fact in the record; sha ordering is not.

    Fleet-name fields are intentionally absent. They are optional audit metadata,
    not review authority.
    """
    out = []
    grammar = _load_grammar()
    eligible = grammar.marker_lines
    for pos, (_cid, body) in enumerate(items):
        for raw in eligible(body or ""):
            line = grammar.strip_decoration(raw)
            assertion_starts = grammar.marker_assertion_starts(line)
            if len(assertion_starts) != 1:
                continue
            assertion_start = assertion_starts[0]
            idx = line.upper().find(APPROVAL, assertion_start)
            if idx != assertion_start:
                continue
            # A withdrawal line contains no APPROVED-AT, and a refusal line
            # cannot: the tokens do not overlap. No longest-first needed.
            tail = line[idx + len(APPROVAL):]
            m = _SHA.search(tail)
            if m is None and grammar.is_template(tail):
                continue
            lane_m = _LANE.search(tail[:24])
            triple = (APPROVAL, lane_m.group(1).lower() if lane_m else None,
                      m.group(1) if m else None)
            if triple[1] is not None and triple[2] is not None:
                out.append((triple[1], triple[2], _cid, pos))
    return out

def markers_with_ids(
    items: list[tuple[object, str]],
) -> list[tuple[str, str | None, str | None, object]]:
    """Every marker-shaped line, as (kind, lane-or-None, sha-or-None), in document order.

    After the shared Markdown normalization, a marker assertion starts the line or
    follows an existing lane heading or ``VERDICT:`` prefix. Marker text inside
    arbitrary prose is a mention. This is the same boundary used by
    approval_binding's malformed-source report, so a full SHA no longer turns a
    mention into a verdict and an absent SHA no longer hides a standalone assertion.
    """
    found = []
    grammar = _load_grammar()
    eligible = grammar.marker_lines
    for _cid, body in items:
        for raw in eligible(body or ""):
            line = grammar.strip_decoration(raw)
            assertion_starts = grammar.marker_assertion_starts(line)
            if not assertion_starts:
                continue
            if len(assertion_starts) != 1:
                # More than one marker under one explicit VERDICT context is
                # ambiguous. Refuse rather than selecting whichever happens to
                # occur first; approval processing above grants nothing here.
                if grammar.refusal_shaped(line):
                    found.append((REFUSAL, None, None, _cid))
                continue
            assertion_start = assertion_starts[0]
            upper = line.upper()
            # Longest first: WITHDRAWN contains REFUSAL, which contains REFUSAL_BARE.
            # A bare hit is recorded AS a refusal -- the downstream binding,
            # withdrawal and indeterminate logic is deliberately unchanged, so the
            # suffix-less spelling gets exactly the treatment the canonical one
            # gets and nothing special.
            for kind in (WITHDRAWN, WITHDRAWN_BARE, REFUSAL, REFUSAL_BARE):
                idx = upper.find(kind, assertion_start)
                if idx != assertion_start:
                    continue
                tail = line[idx + len(kind):]
                m = _SHA.search(tail)
                if m is None and grammar.is_template(tail):
                    break  # a template, not a verdict
                lane_m = _LANE.search(tail[:24])
                triple = (REFUSAL if kind == REFUSAL_BARE
                          else WITHDRAWN if kind == WITHDRAWN_BARE else kind,
                          lane_m.group(1).lower() if lane_m else None,
                          m.group(1) if m else None,
                          _cid)
                found.append(triple)
                break
            else:
                # ⚠️ NO CANONICAL TOKEN ON THIS LINE -- BUT A REFUSAL THAT OMITS THE
                # `-AT` IS STILL A REFUSAL, AND UNTIL NOW THIS GATE COULD NOT SEE
                # ONE. `line.find(REFUSAL)` matches the literal
                # `CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT`, so `CHANGES-REQUESTED: claude <sha>` --
                # which the grammar reports MALFORMED and every human reads as a
                # block -- entered no branch here and the merge proceeded.
                # gh-merge-verified refuses it since `7db64676`; this path did not,
                # and this path is the one `land-pr.sh` uses. Measured on
                # https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2611:
                # `CHANGES-REQUESTED: claude 7a75909c92ae` at the exact head.
                #
                # The same shared grammar decides whether this line is asserting a
                # marker and whether its spelling is refusal-shaped, so the two
                # landers cannot drift apart on that boundary again.
                near = grammar.near_miss_refusal(line, assertion_start)
                if near is None or near.start() != assertion_start:
                    continue
                tail = line[near.end():]
                if grammar.is_template(tail):
                    continue
                m = _SHA.search(tail)
                lane_m = _LANE.search(tail[:24])
                # A standalone assertion with neither lane nor SHA is still
                # unbindable and must fail closed. Ordinary prose such as
                # "Changes requested, on the value pin" never reaches this branch:
                # `marker_assertion` requires the marker's own punctuation.
                triple = (REFUSAL,
                          lane_m.group(1).lower() if lane_m else None,
                          m.group(1) if m else None,
                          _cid)
                found.append(triple)
    return found


def head_is_main_equal(source: str, head: str) -> bool:
    """Does `head` carry nothing over origin/main in `source`?

    ⚠️ A WIPED BRANCH IS NOT A CLEAN HEAD, AND IT LAUNDERS EVERY REFUSAL. Something
    on this box closes pull requests and RESETS BRANCHES to main-equal on a ~10
    minute cadence -- hermit#2448 four times. When that happens the head sha MOVES,
    so every refusal naming the old sha stops binding. This gate used to report its
    most reassuring verdict: "refusal marker(s) present but none names <head> --
    superseded by a later push, the correct clearing path". That sentence is equally
    true of an author pushing a fix and of a branch wiped out from under the review,
    and comments alone cannot tell them apart. Old-head refusals now block until they
    are answered, so a wipe cannot clear them by moving the SHA.

    A main-equal head also merges as a NO-OP, so the pull request closes as "merged"
    while its content never landed -- a landing verified before a wipe looks identical
    to one that reverted itself.

    FAILS SAFE TOWARD NOT-WIPED: if git cannot answer, this returns False and the
    verdict is unchanged, because inventing a wipe would refuse honest heads. The
    check is skipped entirely without --source, which the caller states rather than
    hiding. Measured 2026-08-25: 0 of 28 open heads were main-equal, so this costs
    nothing today and exists for the state the wipe leaves behind.
    """
    try:
        subprocess.run(["git", "-C", source, "fetch", "-q", "--force", "origin",
                        "refs/heads/main:refs/tmp/crg/main"],
                       capture_output=True, timeout=120, check=False)
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", source, "diff", "--name-only",
                            "refs/tmp/crg/main", head],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return False
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return False
    return r.stdout.strip() == ""


def state_verdict(state: str | None) -> tuple[int, str] | None:
    """Decide from the pull request's STATE alone, before any marker is read.

    Returns None when the pull request is OPEN and the marker logic should run.

    ⚠ THIS GATE WAS STATE-BLIND AND SAID SO CONFIDENTLY. Measured on
    hermit#2677: merged 2026-08-26T01:37:12Z, a claude refusal posted 01:41:15Z,
    and the gate then reported

        BLOCKED pr=2677 head=7e3004480dbd -- 1 refusal(s) bound to the exact head
        ... This clears when the AUTHOR pushes a fix (the sha moves ...)

    on a pull request that had been merged for four minutes. The remedy it
    prescribes cannot be performed: there is no branch to push to. A lander polling
    it would see BLOCKED forever, and -- the reason this is worse than noise -- an
    agent citing "the gate went CLEAR then BLOCKED" as proof its marker bound gets
    a TRUE answer about a head that can no longer change.

    ⚠ AND THE CLEAR DIRECTION IS THE SAME DEFECT. A merged pull request with no
    markers used to return CLEAR, which reads as "nothing refuses this, go ahead"
    about something already done. Both directions are state-blindness; only one of
    them looks alarming, which is why only one was noticed.

    Same shape as the fix in `ci-hub/landing/land-pr.sh`, which reads `state`
    beside `headRefName` in one query and refuses anything that is not OPEN --
    including an unreadable state, because an unreadable state is not an open one.
    """
    if state is None or not str(state).strip():
        return (
            INDETERMINATE,
            "the pull request state is unreadable, and an unreadable state is not an "
            "OPEN one. Refusing to evaluate markers against a head whose pull request "
            "may already be merged or closed.",
        )
    st = str(state).strip().upper()
    if st == "OPEN":
        return None
    return (
        MOOT,
        f"the pull request is {st}, not OPEN, so the question this gate answers no "
        f"longer applies. Markers are NOT evaluated: on a {st.lower()} pull request a "
        f"refusal cannot be satisfied (there is no branch to push to) and an absence "
        f"of refusals is not permission to merge something already resolved. If a "
        f"finding arrived after the merge it is real but unbindable -- fix it forward "
        f"on a new head rather than treating this verdict as a block.",
    )


def evaluate(
    bodies: list[str],
    head: str,
    ids: list[object] | None = None,
    require_answered: bool = False,
    native_refusal_ids: set[str] | None = None,
    records: list[dict] | None = None,
) -> tuple[int, str]:
    # `ids` are the comment ids the bodies came from, so a withdrawal can name the
    # refusal it retires. Optional and positional-compatible: every existing caller
    # and test passes bodies alone and gets the previous behaviour.
    grammar = _load_grammar()
    if records is not None:
        try:
            ordered_records = grammar.canonical_review_records(records)
            records_by_id = {
                record_id: record
                for record in ordered_records
                if (record_id := grammar.comment_database_id(record)) is not None
            }
            non_refusal_targets = sorted(
                {
                    claim[0]
                    for record in ordered_records
                    if (claim := grammar.retirement_claim(
                        str(record.get("body") or "")
                    ))
                    is not None
                    and claim[0] in records_by_id
                    and not grammar.review_record_refusals(records_by_id[claim[0]])
                }
            )
            if non_refusal_targets:
                return (
                    BLOCKED,
                    "A `RETIRES` NAMED A COMMENT THAT DOES NOT CONTAIN A REFUSAL, "
                    "so it retired nothing: "
                    + ", ".join(f"comment {target}" for target in non_refusal_targets),
                )
            unresolved = grammar.unresolved_review_objections(
                ordered_records, head
            )
            accepted = grammar.accepted_exact_retirements(
                ordered_records, head
            )
        except grammar.ReviewRecordError as error:
            return (
                INDETERMINATE,
                f"review event order is unreadable: {error}. A retirement cannot "
                "be credited without the immutable refusal and withdrawal times",
            )
        if unresolved:
            attempted_retirements = sum(
                1
                for record in ordered_records
                if grammar.contains_retires_assertion(
                    str(record.get("body") or "")
                )
            )
            unbindable = [item for item in unresolved if not item.get("sha")]
            if unbindable:
                return (
                    INDETERMINATE,
                    f"{len(unbindable)} refusal record(s) have no exact reviewed head "
                    "(no readable sha); "
                    "refusing because an unreadable binding is not a resolved objection",
                )
            current = [
                item
                for item in unresolved
                if grammar.sha_names_same_head(str(item.get("sha") or ""), head)
            ]
            if current:
                retirement_note = ""
                if accepted:
                    retirement_note = (
                        f"; {len(accepted)} other refusal(s) WERE answered by exact "
                        "authorized RETIRES evidence"
                    )
                elif attempted_retirements:
                    retirement_note = (
                        "; the supplied RETIRES record WAS NOT AUTHORIZED"
                    )
                return (
                    BLOCKED,
                    f"{len(current)} unresolved refusal(s) bind the exact head "
                    f"{head[:12]}; a later canonical current-head withdrawal "
                    "or exact authorized RETIRES evidence is required"
                    + retirement_note,
                )
            detail = ", ".join(
                f"{item.get('lane') or '<no lane>'}@"
                f"{str(item.get('sha') or '<no sha>')[:12]}"
                for item in unresolved
            )
            return (
                BLOCKED,
                f"{len(unresolved)} refusal(s) were OUTRUN, not answered; a later "
                "canonical current-head approval or withdrawal, or exact authorized "
                "RETIRES evidence is required: "
                f"{detail}"
                + (
                    "; "
                    + f"{len(accepted)} other refusal(s) WERE answered: "
                    + "; ".join(
                        f"comment {item['target']} was retired by authorized "
                        f"GitHub actor {item['retired_by']}"
                        for item in accepted
                    )
                    if accepted
                    else ""
                ),
            )
        if accepted:
            details = "; ".join(
                f"comment {item['target']} was retired by authorized GitHub actor "
                f"{item['retired_by']}"
                for item in accepted
            )
            return (
                OK,
                f"{len(accepted)} refusal(s) were ANSWERED by later exact authorized "
                f"RETIRES evidence: {details}",
            )
        return OK, "no unresolved review objection is recorded"

    items = list(zip(ids, bodies)) if ids is not None else list(enumerate(bodies))
    ordered_records = [
        {"id": record_id, "body": body}
        for record_id, body in items
    ]
    found4 = markers_with_ids(items)
    native_ids = native_refusal_ids or set()
    refusal_ids_already_found = {
        str(cid) for kind, _lane, _sha, cid in found4 if kind == REFUSAL
    }
    for cid, _body in items:
        if str(cid) in native_ids and str(cid) not in refusal_ids_already_found:
            # Native review state is itself a refusal even when its prose carries
            # no project marker. Its exact review id supplies the retirement target.
            found4.append((REFUSAL, None, None, cid))
    approvals = approvals_in_order(items)
    position_by_id = {cid: pos for pos, (cid, _body) in enumerate(items)}
    found = [(k, l, sh) for k, l, sh, _ in found4]

    # ⚠️ A WITHDRAWAL RETIRES THE REFUSAL IT NAMES, AND ONLY THAT ONE.
    # `RETIRES <comment-id>` in a withdrawal's body retires the refusal carried by
    # that comment. Refusals not named by any withdrawal remain independently
    # addressable.
    #
    # An UNQUOTED withdrawal keeps the previous lane-wide behaviour rather than
    # retiring nothing. Retiring nothing is the stricter reading and it fails
    # closed, but it would flip every live head carrying an existing unquoted
    # withdrawal to BLOCKED at once. The lane-wide path already REPORTS a
    # one-for-many clearance, so the ambiguous case is visible while the precise
    # spelling is adopted.
    # `RETIRES` is the precise record that one named objection was verified resolved.
    # It is deliberately NOT bound to the original issuer: a departed issuer cannot
    # make an answered objection permanent. Authority instead comes from the resolving
    # comment itself: one canonical withdrawal, one exact target, the refusal's lane,
    # the current head, and independently fetched current repository permission
    # for the immutable GitHub actor. Fleet-name and role fields are audit metadata
    # only. The comment's repository and pull request are fixed by this invocation.
    by_id: dict[str, str] = {}
    records_by_id: dict[str, list[dict]] = {}
    for record in ordered_records:
        cid = grammar.comment_database_id(record)
        if cid is None:
            continue
        body = str(record.get("body") or "")
        by_id.setdefault(str(cid), body)
        records_by_id.setdefault(str(cid), []).append(record)
    refusals_by_id: dict[str, list[tuple[str | None, str | None]]] = {}
    for kind, refusal_lane, refusal_sha, cid in found4:
        if kind == REFUSAL and (
            str(cid) in native_ids
            or grammar.comment_contains_refusal(by_id.get(str(cid), ""))
        ):
            refusals_by_id.setdefault(str(cid), []).append((refusal_lane, refusal_sha))
    _retire_claims: dict[str, list[dict]] = {}
    _invalid_retire_claims: dict[str, str] = {}
    for record in ordered_records:
        cid = grammar.comment_database_id(record)
        if cid is None:
            continue
        body = str(record.get("body") or "")
        targets = grammar.retirement_targets(body)
        claim = grammar.retirement_claim(body)
        if claim is not None:
            _retire_claims.setdefault(claim[0], []).append(record)
        elif targets:
            for target in targets:
                _invalid_retire_claims[target] = str(cid)
    # (target comment id) -> reportable exact-resolution details.
    _retire_non_refusal: dict[str, str] = {}
    _retire_ambiguous: dict[str, str] = {}
    _retire_binding_mismatch: dict[str, str] = {}

    def _claimants(key: str) -> str:
        actors = {
            grammar.review_record_actor(record)
            for record in _retire_claims[key]
        }
        return ", ".join(sorted(actor for actor in actors if actor)) or "<unknown GitHub actor>"

    def retired(cid: object) -> bool:
        """Did an authorized current-head withdrawal name this refusal?"""
        key = str(cid)
        if key not in _retire_claims:
            return False
        records = refusals_by_id.get(key, [])
        if not records:
            _retire_non_refusal[key] = _claimants(key)
            return False
        if len(records) != 1:
            _retire_ambiguous[key] = _claimants(key)
            return False
        matching_records = records_by_id.get(key, [])
        if len(matching_records) != 1:
            _retire_ambiguous[key] = _claimants(key)
            return False
        refusal_lane, _refusal_sha = records[0]
        if grammar.retirement_record_allowed(
            matching_records[0], refusal_lane, head, _retire_claims[key]
        ):
            return True
        _retire_binding_mismatch[key] = _claimants(key)
        return False

    def retire_note() -> str:
        parts = []
        if _retire_non_refusal:
            parts.append(
                " ⚠️ A `RETIRES` NAMED A COMMENT THAT DOES NOT CONTAIN A REFUSAL, "
                "so it retired nothing: "
                + "; ".join(
                    f"comment {key} was named by {claimants}"
                    for key, claimants in sorted(_retire_non_refusal.items())
                )
                + ". Marker discovery remains fail-closed, but retirement requires "
                "positive evidence that the named comment actually issued a refusal."
            )
        if _retire_ambiguous:
            parts.append(
                " ⚠️ A `RETIRES` NAMED A COMMENT WITH MORE THAN ONE REFUSAL, so it "
                "retired nothing: "
                + "; ".join(
                    f"comment {key} was named by {claimants}"
                    for key, claimants in sorted(_retire_ambiguous.items())
                )
                + ". Record one exact retirement for one exact refusing comment."
            )
        if _retire_binding_mismatch:
            parts.append(
                " ⚠️ A `RETIRES` RECORD DID NOT FOLLOW THE CURRENT VERSION OF "
                "THE NAMED REFUSAL OR DID NOT MATCH ITS LANE AND THE CURRENT HEAD, "
                "so it retired nothing: "
                + "; ".join(
                    f"comment {key} was named by {claimants}"
                    for key, claimants in sorted(_retire_binding_mismatch.items())
                )
                + ". Read the current refusal, then post a new canonical withdrawal "
                "in its lane at the current repaired head."
            )
        if _invalid_retire_claims:
            parts.append(
                " ⚠️ A `RETIRES` RECORD WAS NOT AUTHORIZED, so it retired nothing: "
                + "; ".join(
                    f"target {key} was named in comment {source}"
                    for key, source in sorted(_invalid_retire_claims.items())
                )
                + ". A precise retirement requires one canonical withdrawal, one exact "
                "target, the refusal's lane, the current head, and current repository permission "
                "for the immutable GitHub actor."
            )
        return "".join(parts)

    if not found:
        return OK, "no refusal marker of any shape on this pull request"

    def binds(sha: str | None) -> bool:
        # Abbreviated shas are normal in these markers; bind by prefix, both ways.
        return bool(sha) and (head.startswith(sha) or sha.startswith(head))

    def answered_by_later_current_marker(
        refusal_lane: str | None,
        refusal_sha: str | None,
        refusal_id: object,
    ) -> bool:
        """Whether lane chronology records a substantive current-head answer."""
        if refusal_lane is None:
            return False
        position = position_by_id.get(refusal_id)
        if position is None:
            return False
        if any(
            approval_lane == refusal_lane
            and approval_position > position
            and binds(approval_sha)
            and not binds(refusal_sha)
            for approval_lane, approval_sha, _approval_id, approval_position
            in approvals
        ):
            return True
        return any(
            kind == WITHDRAWN
            and withdrawal_lane == refusal_lane
            and position_by_id.get(withdrawal_id, -1) > position
            and binds(withdrawal_sha)
            and not grammar.contains_retires_assertion(
                by_id.get(str(withdrawal_id), "")
            )
            for kind, withdrawal_lane, withdrawal_sha, withdrawal_id in found4
        )

    # A laneless refusal remains unresolved. A canonical later decision can
    # discharge only an objection whose lane it matches.
    refusals = [(lane, sha) for kind, lane, sha in found if kind == REFUSAL]
    # Same set, carrying the comment id, so an UNBINDABLE refusal can still be
    # matched against `RETIRES <comment-id>`. See the unbindable check below.
    refusals4 = [(lane, sha, cid) for kind, lane, sha, cid in found4
                 if kind == REFUSAL]

    # ⚠️ A DEFINITE ANSWER OUTRANKS AN UNREADABLE ONE. Both block, so safety does
    # not depend on the order -- but reporting INDETERMINATE when a refusal is
    # demonstrably bound to this head describes the pull request wrongly, and the
    # author is then told to re-post a marker when what they actually need is a fix
    # or a withdrawal. Measured on hermit#2176: a bound refusal AND a sha-less
    # mention coexist, and checking unbindable first reported the weaker verdict.
    bound4 = [(lane, sha, cid) for kind, lane, sha, cid in found4
              if kind == REFUSAL and binds(sha)]
    bound = [(lane, sha) for lane, sha, _ in bound4]
    # A refusal remains live until later same-lane exact-head evidence answers it,
    # or an authorized exact RETIRES record names that one objection. Optional
    # fleet-name metadata does not participate in either decision.
    live = [
        (lane, sha)
        for lane, sha, cid in bound4
        if not retired(cid)
        and not answered_by_later_current_marker(lane, sha, cid)
    ]
    cleared = len(bound) - len(live)

    if bound and not live:
        return OK, (
            f"{cleared} refusal(s) named {head[:12]} and every objection has a "
            "later canonical current-head lane decision or exact authorized "
            "RETIRES record"
            + retire_note()
        )
    if live:
        lanes = ",".join(sorted({lane or "unlaned" for lane, _ in live}))
        note = (
            f" ({cleared} other refusal(s) answered by later exact-head evidence)"
            if cleared else ""
        ) + retire_note()
        withdrawal_lanes = {
            lane
            for kind, lane, sha, _comment_id in found4
            if kind == WITHDRAWN and lane is not None and binds(sha)
        }
        cross_lane_note = (
            " A withdrawal in a different lane does not answer this objection."
            if withdrawal_lanes - {lane for lane, _sha in live if lane is not None}
            else ""
        )
        missing_records_note = (
            " A RETIRES claim is present but no review records were supplied, so "
            "the exact retirement cannot be checked. Supply complete review records "
            "and evaluate again; this is missing evidence, not a rejected claim."
            if records is None
            and any(grammar.contains_retires_assertion(body) for body in bodies)
            else ""
        )
        return (
            BLOCKED,
            f"{len(live)} refusal(s) bound to the exact head {head[:12]} from "
            f"lane(s) {lanes} that have no later canonical decision{note}. A refusal "
            "naming the head blocks until a later canonical withdrawal in that lane "
            "at the same exact head, or an exact authorized RETIRES record, says the "
            "objection is resolved. A same-head approval is conflicting evidence, "
            f"not a withdrawal.{cross_lane_note}{missing_records_note}",
        )

    # ⚠️ A SHA-LESS REFUSAL USED TO BE UNRETIRABLE BY ANY MEANS, WHICH MADE IT A
    # PERMANENT BLOCK. It names no sha, so pushing never stops it naming the head;
    # and the withdrawal machinery above runs only over `bound4` -- refusals that
    # DO name the head -- so nothing sha-less ever reached it. Measured before this
    # change, against a bare refusal plus a withdrawal in five spellings (bare,
    # lane-only, lane+head-sha, and an explicit `RETIRES <id>`): all five returned
    # INDETERMINATE, while the same withdrawal cleared a well-formed refusal from
    # BLOCKED to OK. So the remedy this very message printed -- "or withdraw it" --
    # could not be carried out, and the only real exit was editing someone else's
    # comment.
    #
    # `RETIRES <comment-id>` is honoured here and the blunt forms are NOT, on
    # purpose. It names exactly one comment, so it cannot over-clear, and it does
    # not depend on the disclosure tag being present -- which it is not, roughly a
    # third of the time. A lane-wide or bare withdrawal stays powerless against a
    # marker that never said which head it was about.
    unbindable = [sha for lane, sha, cid in refusals4
                  if sha is None and not retired(cid)]
    if unbindable:
        return (
            INDETERMINATE,
            f"{len(unbindable)} refusal marker(s) carry no readable sha, so whether "
            f"they bind {head[:12]} cannot be decided. Refusing: a marker that cannot "
            f"be bound is not a marker that does not apply. Re-post it as "
            f"'{REFUSAL}: <lane> <40-hex>' on its own line, or retire it with a "
            f"'{WITHDRAWN}' comment carrying 'RETIRES <the refusing comment id>' and "
            f"current repository permission -- a bare or lane-only withdrawal "
            f"CANNOT retire a marker that names no head."
            + retire_note(),
        )

    # ⚠️ ANSWERED AND OUTRUN ARE NOT THE SAME EVENT, AND THIS USED TO REPORT BOTH AS
    # "superseded by a later push, the correct clearing path". That sentence is true
    # of the MECHANISM and unsupported as a statement about any particular head:
    # pushing is how an author addresses a refusal, and it is equally how an author
    # walks past one. The gate knew only that the sha had moved and asserted the
    # benign reading of an ambiguity it had not resolved.
    #
    # ⚠️ WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN WORDING. The clearing rule is what makes a refusal
    # cheap to issue. If any push discharges one, a reviewer's only durable options
    # are to re-review every later sha forever or to stop refusing. Measured
    # 2026-08-26 on hermit#2668: FIVE refusals were discharged by one reading, two of
    # them substantive, and the readout distinguished none of them from a refusal
    # nobody had answered. That is the reader's half of the 23 orphaned refusals --
    # they went unnoticed because this line rendered both cases identically.
    #
    # ⚠️ AND ANSWERED IS A STATEMENT ABOUT THE RECORD'S SHAPE, NOT ABOUT THE FIX.
    # This is the one way a verdict loses its meaning that leaves no trace here.
    # Every other one is visible to a reader of the record: a refusal outrun by a
    # push has no withdrawal, a stale label names an older sha, a malformed line
    # fails to parse, a cross-lane withdrawal comes from the wrong lane. Each is an
    # ABSENCE or a MISMATCH that something can point at.
    #
    # A refusal DISCHARGED BY FIXING THE WRONG THING is none of those. The refusing
    # lane really did withdraw, at the sha it really did refuse, after a change the
    # author really did make -- so it renders as the ideal case, and it is the
    # strongest ANSWERED signal this gate can emit. The objection was addressed in
    # FORM; the finding is untouched.
    #
    # Measured 2026-08-26 on hermit#2672: a reviewer summarising a live claude
    # refusal described it as blocking on "the reserved LiteInst runtime", which
    # that lane had explicitly excluded under LIMITS as a debug-profile artifact
    # present in its own unmutated control. The actual blocker was an untested
    # producer. Had the author fixed the LiteInst staging and the lane withdrawn on
    # that summary, this branch would have printed the sentence above and the
    # untested producer would have landed. It was caught only because the refusing
    # lane re-read its own words, which is not a mechanism.
    #
    # NOT DETECTABLE HERE, and no check is proposed: deciding whether a delta
    # addresses a finding is the review, and a gate that claimed to do it would be
    # the ninth failure mode wearing a green tick. What this gate can stop doing is
    # ASSERTING the benign reading, which is the same correction ANSWERED-vs-OUTRUN
    # made one level up -- so the verdict above states the form and declines the
    # inference. The reader's remedy is the cheap one: a withdrawal should quote the
    # finding it believes was addressed, so a summary that drifted is visible next
    # to the thing it summarised.
    #
    # A refusal is ANSWERED only by a later canonical decision in its own lane at
    # the exact current head, or by authorized exact RETIRES evidence. A different
    # lane, an earlier record, a stale head, or malformed marker cannot discharge it.
    # Fleet-name metadata is deliberately irrelevant to this classification.
    answered, outrun = [], []
    for kind, lane, sha, cid in found4:
        if kind != REFUSAL:
            continue
        if retired(cid) or answered_by_later_current_marker(lane, sha, cid):
            answered.append((lane, sha))
        else:
            outrun.append((lane, sha))

    if not outrun:
        return (
            OK,
            f"{len(refusals)} refusal marker(s) present, none names {head[:12]}, and "
            f"every one was ANSWERED by a later canonical current-head approval or "
            f"withdrawal, or exact authorized RETIRES evidence."
            + retire_note(),
        )

    detail = ", ".join(
        f"{lane or '<no lane>'}@{(sha or '<no sha>')[:12]}"
        for lane, sha in outrun
    )
    return (
        BLOCKED,
        f"{len(outrun)} of {len(refusals)} refusal marker(s) were OUTRUN, not answered: "
        f"{detail}. The sha moved so they no longer name {head[:12]}, and no later "
        f"canonical current-head approval or withdrawal, or exact authorized "
        f"RETIRES record says the objection was resolved." + retire_note(),
    )


_T_HEAD = "f013ac4b1a3155ebede568c5667f915a4c8ff6b1"
_T_REF = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude {_T_HEAD}"
_T_WD = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN-AT: claude {_T_HEAD}"
_T_CODEX_REF = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: codex {_T_HEAD}"
# Two agents in ONE lane -- the shape a per-lane counter could not tell apart.
_A1, _A2 = "hermit-012", "hermit-015"


def _by(agent: str, marker: str) -> str:
    """A comment body carrying a disclosure tag, so its markers have an author."""
    return f"[hermit2, {agent}, unresolved, devbig014, role=reviewer]\n{marker}"


def self_test() -> int:
    """Bracket withdrawal scoping. Both defects it covers were live, not imagined.

    ⚠️ THE MARKER LINES ARE REAL, from hermit#2625 comments c5417453728 and
    c5417519496 (two refusals, SAME LANE, different agents) and c5417606538 (the
    unquoted withdrawal). Same-lane is what makes it a test of the gate: a synthetic
    pair would likely have used two lanes and passed for the wrong reason, which the
    cross-lane fix already covers.
    """
    grammar = _load_grammar()

    def event_records(bodies: list[str], ids: list[object]) -> list[dict]:
        """Current GitHub-shaped records in a known chronological order."""
        return [
            {
                "id": record_id,
                "body": body,
                "user": {"login": "fixture-reviewer"},
                "_review_surface": "issue-comment",
                "_current_review_actor_authorized": True,
                "_current_review_actor_login": "fixture-reviewer",
                "_current_review_actor_permission": "write",
                "created_at": f"2026-09-04T00:00:{position:02d}Z",
                "updated_at": f"2026-09-04T00:00:{position:02d}Z",
            }
            for position, (record_id, body) in enumerate(zip(ids, bodies), 1)
        ]

    cases = [
        ("two same-lane refusals, no withdrawal", [_T_REF, _T_REF], BLOCKED),
        ("one refusal and a current-head withdrawal",
         [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A1, _T_WD)], OK),
        ("two same-lane refusals, ONE unquoted withdrawal",
         [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A2, _T_REF), _by(_A1, _T_WD)], OK),
        ("different fleet-name metadata does not prevent withdrawal",
         [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A2, _T_WD)], OK),
        ("bare RETIRES attempt cannot fall back to generic withdrawal",
         [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A1, f"{_T_WD}\nRETIRES")], BLOCKED),
        ("non-numeric RETIRES attempt cannot fall back to generic withdrawal",
         [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A1, f"{_T_WD}\nRETIRES nope")], BLOCKED),
        ("hash non-numeric RETIRES attempt cannot fall back to generic withdrawal",
         [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A1, f"{_T_WD}\nRETIRES #not-a-number")], BLOCKED),
        ("missing withdrawal metadata does not prevent discharge",
         [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _T_WD], OK),
        ("missing refusal metadata does not prevent discharge",
         [_T_REF, _by(_A1, _T_WD)], OK),
        # Guards the cross-lane fix that landed before this one.
        ("claude withdrawal must not clear a CODEX refusal", [_T_CODEX_REF, _T_WD], BLOCKED),
    ]
    bad = []
    for name, bodies, want in cases:
        got, why = evaluate(bodies, _T_HEAD)
        if got != want:
            bad.append(f"{name}: got {got}, want {want} -- {why[:80]}")

    # Fleet names are optional metadata. Changing or omitting them must not alter
    # the result or the authoritative explanation. Agent-utils PR #117 owns the
    # corresponding metadata normalization at its publication boundary; these
    # controls cover only this gate's authority decision.
    _tag = lambda who, line: f"[hermit2, {who}, unresolved, devbig014, role=reviewer]\n{line}"
    decidable = [
        _tag("hermit-012", _T_REF),
        _tag("hermit-015", _T_REF),
        _tag("hermit-012", _T_WD),
    ]
    named_rc, named_why = evaluate(decidable, _T_HEAD)
    if named_rc != OK:
        bad.append(f"named metadata: got {named_rc}, want {OK}")

    # THE REAL hermit#2625 SHAPE: of the two same-lane refusals at f013ac4b,
    # hermit-012's carries a disclosure tag and hermit-015's carries NONE. The one
    # that must keep binding is exactly the one with no readable issuer, so this
    # must fail CLOSED rather than guess.
    unreadable = [_tag("hermit-012", _T_REF), _T_REF, _tag("hermit-012", _T_WD)]
    unnamed_rc, unnamed_why = evaluate(unreadable, _T_HEAD)
    if unnamed_rc != OK:
        bad.append(f"unnamed metadata: got {unnamed_rc}, want {OK}")
    if named_why != unnamed_why:
        bad.append("fleet-name metadata changed the refusal verdict")

    # ⚠️ ANSWERED MUST RENDER DIFFERENTLY FROM OUTRUN. Both are "no refusal binds
    # this head", both were reported as "superseded by a later push, the correct
    # clearing path", and that single sentence is why 23 orphaned refusals went
    # unnoticed. These cases assert both the distinction and the exit code: an
    # answered refusal clears, while an outrun refusal blocks the production path.
    _MOVED = "a" * 40
    _MOVED_WD = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN-AT: claude {_MOVED}"
    render = [
        (
            "a stale-head withdrawal does not answer a refusal",
            [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A1, _T_WD)], BLOCKED, "OUTRUN", None,
        ),
        (
            "a refusal merely OUTRUN by a later push, never withdrawn",
            [_T_REF], BLOCKED, "OUTRUN", "ANSWERED",
        ),
        (
            "a CODEX refusal outrun while a CLAUDE one was answered",
            [_T_CODEX_REF, _by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A1, _T_WD)],
            BLOCKED, "OUTRUN", None,
        ),
    ]
    for name, bodies, want_rc, must_say, must_not_say in render:
        rc, why = evaluate(bodies, _MOVED)
        if must_say not in why:
            bad.append(f"{name}: verdict does not say {must_say} -- {why[:90]}")
        if must_not_say and must_not_say in why.split(".")[0]:
            bad.append(f"{name}: verdict wrongly leads with {must_not_say} -- {why[:90]}")
        if rc != want_rc:
            bad.append(f"{name}: default rc is {rc}, want {want_rc}")

    # ⚠️ A BOTCHED WITHDRAWAL MUST NOT MANUFACTURE A REFUSAL. The suffix-less
    # spelling used to miss WITHDRAWN and REFUSAL and land on REFUSAL_BARE, so an
    # agent following the remediation text and mistyping it made the head MORE
    # blocked. Measured 2026-08-26 before the fix: the line ALONE returned
    # BLOCKED, and beside a real refusal it produced "2 refusal(s) ... from
    # lane(s) claude,unlaned".
    _T_WD_BARE = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN {_T_HEAD}"
    if evaluate([_T_WD_BARE], _T_HEAD)[0] != OK:
        bad.append("a botched withdrawal ALONE still blocks -- it manufactured a refusal")
    _rc, _why = evaluate([_T_REF, _T_WD_BARE], _T_HEAD)
    if _rc != BLOCKED:
        bad.append("a real refusal stopped blocking when a botched withdrawal joined it")
    if "2 refusal" in _why:
        bad.append(f"the botched withdrawal is still counted AS a refusal -- {_why[:70]}")
    # ⚠️ AND IT MUST NOT CLEAR EITHER. An unreadable withdrawal waving a real
    # refusal through is the opposite failure and the more dangerous one; this
    # spelling carries no lane, so it is inert in both directions.
    if evaluate([_T_REF, _T_WD_BARE], _T_HEAD)[0] != BLOCKED:
        bad.append("a botched withdrawal CLEARED a real refusal -- wrong polarity")

    # A withdrawal bound to the refused head is stale at the new head, so it does
    # not answer the objection there.
    stale_rc, stale_why = evaluate([_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A1, _T_WD)], _MOVED)
    _, outrun_why = evaluate([_T_REF], _MOVED)
    if stale_rc != BLOCKED or stale_why != outrun_why:
        bad.append("a stale-head withdrawal changed the unresolved-refusal verdict")
    current_rc, current_why = evaluate(
        [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A2, _MOVED_WD)], _MOVED
    )
    if current_rc != OK or "ANSWERED" not in current_why:
        bad.append("a later current-head withdrawal did not answer the objection")

    # The compatibility option keeps the same exact-head chronology.
    if evaluate([_T_REF], _MOVED, require_answered=True)[0] != BLOCKED:
        bad.append("--require-answered did not block an outrun refusal")
    if evaluate(
        [_by(_A1, _T_REF), _by(_A2, _MOVED_WD)],
        _MOVED,
        require_answered=True,
    )[0] != OK:
        bad.append("--require-answered blocked a current-head withdrawal")
    rc, why = evaluate([_T_REF, _T_WD], _MOVED, require_answered=True)
    if rc != BLOCKED or "OUTRUN" not in why:
        bad.append(
            "a stale-head withdrawal cleared an old-head refusal "
            f"-- rc={rc} msg={why[:110]}"
        )

    # The exact old-head shape that falsely cleared hermit#2670: a precise
    # withdrawal for an unsigned refusal shares the same lane and refused SHA as
    # a named refusal.  The precise retirement clears only its target; it must not
    # also act as an unquoted withdrawal for the named refusal.
    _R1_ID, _R2_ID, _W_ID = 900001, 900002, 900003
    old_head_bodies = [
        _by(_A1, _T_REF),
        _T_REF,
        _by(_A2, f"{_MOVED_WD}\nRETIRES {_R2_ID}"),
    ]
    old_head_ids = [_R1_ID, _R2_ID, _W_ID]
    rc, why = evaluate(
        old_head_bodies,
        _MOVED,
        ids=old_head_ids,
        require_answered=True,
        records=event_records(old_head_bodies, old_head_ids),
    )
    if (
        rc != BLOCKED
        or "OUTRUN" not in why
        or f"comment {_R2_ID} was retired by authorized GitHub actor fixture-reviewer" not in why
    ):
        bad.append(
            "an old-head precise retirement of an unsigned refusal cleared a named "
            f"peer at the same lane and SHA -- rc={rc} msg={why[:110]}"
        )
    unsigned_old_head = [
        _T_REF,
        _by(_A2, f"{_MOVED_WD}\nRETIRES {_R2_ID}"),
    ]
    unsigned_old_head_ids = [_R2_ID, _W_ID]
    rc, why = evaluate(
        unsigned_old_head,
        _MOVED,
        ids=unsigned_old_head_ids,
        require_answered=True,
        records=event_records(unsigned_old_head, unsigned_old_head_ids),
    )
    if rc != OK or f"comment {_R2_ID} was retired by authorized GitHub actor fixture-reviewer" not in why:
        bad.append(
            "the exact unsigned old-head refusal did not retain its permitted precise "
            f"exit -- rc={rc} msg={why[:110]}"
        )
    # ⚠ THE STATE GATE, BOTH DIRECTIONS. hermit#2677 merged 2026-08-26T01:37:12Z;
    # a refusal posted 01:41:15Z made this gate print BLOCKED with a remedy nobody
    # could perform. The MERGED cases are that defect; the OPEN case is what stops
    # the fix from swallowing every verdict.
    state_cases = [
        ("OPEN falls through to the marker logic", "OPEN", None),
        ("MERGED is MOOT", "MERGED", MOOT),
        ("CLOSED is MOOT", "CLOSED", MOOT),
        ("state is compared case-insensitively", "merged", MOOT),
        ("unreadable state is INDETERMINATE, not OPEN", None, INDETERMINATE),
        ("empty state is INDETERMINATE, not OPEN", "   ", INDETERMINATE),
    ]
    for name, st, want in state_cases:
        got = state_verdict(st)
        gotrc = None if got is None else got[0]
        if gotrc != want:
            bad.append(f"state: {name}: got {gotrc}, want {want}")

    # ⚠ AND THE CONTROLS THAT MUST FAIL. A `state_verdict` that returned MOOT for
    # everything would satisfy four of the six cases above, and a gate that returned
    # MOOT before reading markers would make every BLOCKED verdict disappear -- the
    # reassuring direction. These assert that an OPEN pull request still reaches the
    # marker logic AND still blocks on a live refusal.
    if state_verdict("OPEN") is not None:
        bad.append("state: OPEN did not fall through; the marker logic would never run")
    if evaluate([_T_REF], _T_HEAD)[0] != BLOCKED:
        bad.append("state: an OPEN pr with a live refusal no longer BLOCKS -- the fix over-reached")
    # land-pr.sh dispatches `0) clear ;; *) abandon ;;`, so a zero MOOT would turn a
    # merged pull request into permission.
    if MOOT == OK:
        bad.append("state: MOOT is zero; land-pr.sh would read a merged pr as clear")

    # `RETIRES` binds to one exact refusal and its lane/head, not to any optional
    # fleet-name metadata. The negative cells keep exact target and permission
    # requirements load-bearing.
    _R_ID, _W_ID = "5420278252", "5420547902"
    _R_REF = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: codex {_T_HEAD}"
    _R_WD = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN-AT: codex {_T_HEAD}"
    _R_MOVED_WD = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN-AT: codex {_MOVED}"
    retires = [
        ("a matching metadata name is accepted",
         [_tag("hermit-005", _R_REF), _tag("hermit-005", f"{_R_WD}\nRETIRES {_R_ID}")],
         OK, None),
        ("a different metadata name is accepted",
         [_tag("hermit-005", _R_REF), _tag("hermit-107", f"{_R_WD}\nRETIRES {_R_ID}")],
         OK, None),
        ("a withdrawal with no fleet name is accepted",
         [_tag("hermit-005", _R_REF), f"{_R_WD}\nRETIRES {_R_ID}"],
         OK, None),
        ("a refusal with no fleet name keeps its exact authorized exit",
         [_R_REF, _tag("hermit-107", f"{_R_WD}\nRETIRES {_R_ID}")],
         OK, None),
        ("RETIRES naming a DIFFERENT comment retires nothing",
         [_tag("hermit-005", _R_REF), _tag("hermit-005", f"{_R_WD}\nRETIRES 5999999999")],
         BLOCKED, None),
        ("a canonical current-head withdrawal clears earlier lane objections",
         [_tag("hermit-005", _R_REF), _tag("hermit-005", _R_WD)],
         OK, None),
    ]
    for name, bodies, want_rc, must_say in retires:
        ids = [_R_ID, _W_ID][: len(bodies)]
        rc, why = evaluate(
            bodies,
            _T_HEAD,
            ids=ids,
            records=event_records(bodies, ids),
        )
        if rc != want_rc:
            bad.append(f"retires: {name} -- rc={rc}, want {want_rc}: {why[:110]}")
        elif must_say and must_say not in why:
            bad.append(f"retires: {name} -- verdict does not say {must_say!r}: {why[:140]}")
    # False, matching, and absent fleet names leave the authoritative result and
    # its explanation unchanged when the immutable GitHub actor is the same.
    audit_bodies = [
        _tag("hermit-005", _R_REF),
        _tag("hermit-107", f"{_R_WD}\nRETIRES {_R_ID}"),
    ]
    audit_ids = [_R_ID, _W_ID]
    named_rc, named_reason = evaluate(
        audit_bodies,
        _T_HEAD,
        ids=audit_ids,
        records=event_records(audit_bodies, audit_ids),
    )
    no_name_bodies = [_R_REF, f"{_R_WD}\nRETIRES {_R_ID}"]
    no_name_rc, no_name_reason = evaluate(
        no_name_bodies,
        _T_HEAD,
        ids=audit_ids,
        records=event_records(no_name_bodies, audit_ids),
    )
    if named_rc != OK or no_name_rc != OK or named_reason != no_name_reason:
        bad.append("retires: fleet-name metadata changed exact-retirement authority")
    for token in (_R_ID, "fixture-reviewer"):
        if token not in named_reason:
            bad.append(f"retires: the retirement verdict does not name {token}")

    # A later current-head canonical approval is a substantive lane decision.
    # Fleet-name metadata and BY clauses do not change it; lane and chronology do.
    _T_OTHER = "c" * 40
    _T_APP = f"APPROVED-AT: claude {_T_OTHER}"
    _T_APP_CODEX = f"APPROVED-AT: codex {_T_OTHER}"
    relane = [
        ([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _tag("hermit-004", _T_APP)],
         OK, "matching metadata name"),
        ([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _tag("hermit-003", _T_APP)],
         OK, "different metadata name"),
        ([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _T_APP], OK, "missing approval metadata"),
        ([_T_REF, _tag("hermit-003", _T_APP)], OK, "missing refusal metadata"),
        ([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _tag("hermit-001", _T_APP_CODEX)],
         BLOCKED, "different lane"),
        ([_tag("hermit-003", _T_APP), _tag("hermit-004", _T_REF)],
         BLOCKED, "earlier approval"),
        ([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF)], BLOCKED, "no approval"),
        ([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _tag("hermit-003", f"{_T_APP} BY hermit-004")],
         OK, "matching BY metadata"),
        ([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _tag("hermit-004", f"{_T_APP} BY hermit-003")],
         OK, "different BY metadata"),
    ]
    for bodies, want, description in relane:
        rc, msg = evaluate(bodies, _T_OTHER)
        if rc != want or (want == BLOCKED and "OUTRUN" not in msg):
            bad.append(f"later approval: {description} -- rc={rc} msg={msg[:110]}")

    # Name changes do not alter either the decision or the explanation.
    different_rc, different_why = evaluate(
        [_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _tag("hermit-003", _T_APP)], _T_OTHER)
    missing_rc, missing_why = evaluate([_T_REF, _T_APP], _T_OTHER)
    if different_why != missing_why:
        bad.append("later approval metadata changed the resolved-refusal verdict")
    if evaluate([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _tag("hermit-003", _T_APP)],
                _T_OTHER)[0] != OK:
        bad.append("the production path blocked an identity-free later approval")
    if evaluate([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _tag("hermit-003", _T_APP)], _T_OTHER,
                require_answered=True)[0] != OK:
        bad.append("--require-answered blocked an identity-free later approval")
    if evaluate([_tag("hermit-004", _T_REF), _tag("hermit-004", _T_APP)], _T_OTHER,
                require_answered=True)[0] != OK:
        bad.append("--require-answered blocked a later current-head approval")

    # ⚠️ ABSENCE MUST BE EXPRESSIBLE, AND A VERDICT MUST REACH STDOUT.
    #
    # These are the cases the old shape could not state. `no_verdict_in` is the
    # only thing standing between a caller and a missing gate, because a missing
    # gate has no code to run: 49 of 76 checkouts measured 2026-08-26, where the
    # shell says `No such file or directory` on stderr and, piped, exits 0.
    #
    # The stdout half is what makes the empty case UNAMBIGUOUS. While BLOCKED went
    # to stderr, an empty stdout meant "clear-and-nothing-to-say", "blocked",
    # "moot", "crashed" or "absent" -- four of which forbid the merge -- so a
    # reader who saw nothing could not act on it. Every verdict on stdout leaves
    # exactly one meaning for silence.
    import contextlib
    import io

    absence = [
        ("empty output has no verdict", "", True),
        # ⚠ THE THREE WAYS ABSENCE ANNOUNCES ITSELF, ALL OF WHICH NAME THIS FILE.
        # These are the cases that killed the prefix-only detector. The third puts
        # the tool's name at column zero followed by a colon and a space -- the
        # exact shape of a verdict line -- so only the LABEL tells them apart.
        ("bash's absence diagnostic is not a verdict",
         "bash: ./ci-hub/bin/changes-requested-gate: No such file or directory", True),
        ("python3's absence diagnostic is not a verdict",
         "python3: can't open file '/w/ci-hub/bin/changes-requested-gate': "
         "[Errno 2] No such file or directory", True),
        ("the tool's name at column zero without a label is not a verdict",
         "changes-requested-gate: No such file or directory", True),
        ("a verdict must be at the start of its line, not quoted inside prose",
         "I ran it and it said changes-requested-gate: CLEAR, honest", True),
        ("a real verdict line is a verdict",
         f"{VERDICT_PREFIX} CLEAR pr=1 head=abc gate=deadbeef -- nothing binds", False),
        ("a BLOCKED line is also a verdict, not an absence",
         f"{VERDICT_PREFIX} BLOCKED pr=1 head=abc gate=deadbeef -- one refusal", False),
        ("None is treated as no output rather than crashing", None, True),
    ]
    for name, out, want in absence:
        if no_verdict_in(out) is not want:
            bad.append(f"absence: {name}: no_verdict_in returned {not want}")

    # EVERY rc REACHES STDOUT. The loop covers all four because the defect was
    # per-outcome: OK was already correct and the other three were not, so a test
    # that only exercised the clear path would have passed throughout.
    for rc in (OK, BLOCKED, INDETERMINATE, MOOT):
        cap_out, cap_err = io.StringIO(), io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(cap_out), contextlib.redirect_stderr(cap_err):
            got_rc = emit(rc, 1, "a" * 40, "why")
        if got_rc != rc:
            bad.append(f"emit: rc {rc} came back as {got_rc}")
        if no_verdict_in(cap_out.getvalue()):
            bad.append(f"emit: {LABELS[rc]} did not reach STDOUT -- a reader piping "
                       f"stdout sees the same nothing as a missing gate")
        if LABELS[rc] not in cap_out.getvalue():
            bad.append(f"emit: {LABELS[rc]} is not named in its own verdict line")
        if cap_err.getvalue().strip():
            bad.append(f"emit: {LABELS[rc]} also wrote to stderr; one verdict, one stream")

    # ⚠ AND THE VERDICT NAMES THE COPY THAT ISSUED IT. Without this a stale gate is
    # indistinguishable from a current one, which is the 20-of-76 half of the same
    # census. `gate=` must never be empty and `gate_provenance` must never raise --
    # a gate that withheld a verdict because it could not describe itself would be
    # a worse failure than the one this repairs.
    cap_out = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(cap_out):
        emit(OK, 1, "a" * 40, "why")
    stamp = cap_out.getvalue()
    if " gate=" not in stamp:
        bad.append("provenance: the verdict line carries no gate= stamp")
    if " gate= " in stamp or stamp.rstrip().endswith("gate="):
        bad.append("provenance: gate= is empty; UNKNOWN is the answer for 'cannot tell'")
    try:
        if not gate_provenance():
            bad.append("provenance: gate_provenance returned an empty string")
    except BaseException as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 -- the point is that it cannot
        bad.append(f"provenance: gate_provenance raised {type(exc).__name__}: {exc}")

    # ⚠️ THE COMMAND PATH, NOT ANOTHER CALL TO evaluate(). The pure function
    # already passed precise comment ids while main() discarded them from every
    # GitHub object. That made the tests green while the installed command could
    # not bind ``RETIRES #<comment-id>`` to the refusal it named. These subprocesses
    # cross argument parsing, JSON ingestion and the call into evaluate, so removing
    # ``ids=ids`` below makes the first case fail for the production reason.
    command_refusal_id = 5420547902
    command_withdrawal_id = 5437102604

    def run_command(records: object) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="changes-requested-gate-") as directory:
            fixture = pathlib.Path(directory) / "comments.json"
            fixture.write_text(json.dumps(records), encoding="utf-8")
            return subprocess.run(
                [
                    sys.executable,
                    str(pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve()),
                    "--repo", "example/repo",
                    "--pr", "2680",
                    "--head", _MOVED,
                    "--from-json", str(fixture),
                ],
                capture_output=True,
                text=True,
                timeout=30,
            )

    command_cases = [
        (
            "an exact comment id retires the unsigned refusal through the command",
            command_refusal_id,
            OK,
            f"comment {command_refusal_id} was retired by authorized GitHub actor hermit-121",
        ),
        (
            "a different comment id leaves the unsigned refusal blocked",
            5999999999,
            BLOCKED,
            "OUTRUN",
        ),
    ]
    for name, target, want_rc, must_say in command_cases:
        result = run_command([
            {
                "id": command_refusal_id,
                "body": _R_REF,
                "user": {"login": "fixture-reviewer"},
                "_review_surface": "issue-comment",
                "_current_review_actor_authorized": True,
                "_current_review_actor_login": "fixture-reviewer",
                "_current_review_actor_permission": "write",
                "created_at": "2026-09-04T00:00:01Z",
                "updated_at": "2026-09-04T00:00:01Z",
            },
            {
                "id": command_withdrawal_id,
                "body": _tag(
                    "hermit-121",
                    f"{_R_MOVED_WD}\nRETIRES #{target}",
                ),
                "user": {"login": "hermit-121"},
                "_review_surface": "issue-comment",
                "_current_review_actor_authorized": True,
                "_current_review_actor_login": "hermit-121",
                "_current_review_actor_permission": "write",
                "created_at": "2026-09-04T00:00:02Z",
                "updated_at": "2026-09-04T00:00:02Z",
            },
        ])
        if result.returncode != want_rc or must_say not in result.stdout:
            bad.append(
                f"command: {name} -- rc={result.returncode}, want {want_rc}; "
                f"stdout={result.stdout[:180]!r}; stderr={result.stderr[:100]!r}"
            )
        if no_verdict_in(result.stdout):
            bad.append(f"command: {name} -- no verdict reached stdout")

    current_refusal = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude {_MOVED}"
    current_approval = f"APPROVED-AT: claude {_MOVED}"
    current_withdrawal = f"CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN-AT: claude {_MOVED}"
    chronology_cases = [
        (
            "a same-head generic approval remains conflicting evidence",
            [current_refusal, current_approval],
            BLOCKED,
            "canonical current-head withdrawal",
        ),
        (
            "an explicit same-head withdrawal answers the objection",
            [current_refusal, current_withdrawal],
            OK,
            "no unresolved review objection is recorded",
        ),
    ]
    for name, bodies, want_rc, must_say in chronology_cases:
        ids = [command_refusal_id, command_withdrawal_id]
        result = run_command(event_records(bodies, ids))
        if result.returncode != want_rc or must_say not in result.stdout:
            bad.append(
                f"command chronology: {name} -- rc={result.returncode}, "
                f"want {want_rc}; stdout={result.stdout[:180]!r}"
            )

    incomplete = run_command([current_refusal, current_approval])
    if (
        incomplete.returncode != INDETERMINATE
        or "body-string input is incomplete" not in incomplete.stdout
    ):
        bad.append(
            "command: body-string --from-json did not refuse incomplete records "
            f"-- rc={incomplete.returncode}; stdout={incomplete.stdout[:180]!r}"
        )

    missing_permission = event_records(
        [current_refusal], [command_refusal_id]
    )
    missing_permission[0].pop("_current_review_actor_authorized")
    missing_permission[0].pop("_current_review_actor_permission")
    result = run_command(missing_permission)
    if (
        result.returncode != INDETERMINATE
        or "explicit current GitHub actor permission annotation" not in result.stdout
    ):
        bad.append(
            "command: object --from-json without permission annotation was not "
            f"INDETERMINATE -- rc={result.returncode}; stdout={result.stdout[:180]!r}"
        )

    unauthorized_refusal = event_records(
        [current_refusal], [command_refusal_id]
    )
    unauthorized_refusal[0].update(
        user={"login": "outside-reader"},
        _current_review_actor_authorized=False,
        _current_review_actor_login="outside-reader",
        _current_review_actor_permission="read",
    )
    result = run_command(unauthorized_refusal)
    if (
        result.returncode != OK
        or "no unresolved review objection is recorded" not in result.stdout
    ):
        bad.append(
            "command: an explicitly unauthorized refusal changed authority "
            f"-- rc={result.returncode}; stdout={result.stdout[:180]!r}"
        )

    tied_records = event_records(
        [f"CHANGES-REQUESTED: claude {_MOVED}", current_approval],
        [command_refusal_id, command_withdrawal_id],
    )
    tied_records[1]["created_at"] = tied_records[0]["created_at"]
    tied_records[1]["updated_at"] = tied_records[0]["updated_at"]
    for records in (tied_records, list(reversed(tied_records))):
        result = run_command(records)
        if (
            result.returncode != INDETERMINATE
            or "opposing review authority has the same version time" not in result.stdout
        ):
            bad.append(
                "command: tied malformed refusal and approval depended on id order "
                f"-- rc={result.returncode}; stdout={result.stdout[:180]!r}"
            )

    native_review = {
        "fullDatabaseId": command_refusal_id,
        "id": "PRR_kwDOExample",
        "state": "CHANGES_REQUESTED",
        "author": {"login": "fixture-reviewer"},
        "_review_surface": "pull-request-review",
        "_current_review_actor_authorized": True,
        "_current_review_actor_login": "fixture-reviewer",
        "_current_review_actor_permission": "write",
        "commit": {"oid": _T_HEAD},
        "body": "Please fix the unchecked failure.",
        "submittedAt": "2026-09-04T00:00:01Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-04T00:00:01Z",
        "lastEditedAt": None,
        "includesCreatedEdit": False,
    }
    native_cases = [
        (
            "markerless native refusal has an exact current-head exit",
            command_refusal_id,
            _R_MOVED_WD,
            OK,
        ),
        (
            "markerless native refusal rejects a stale-head retirement",
            command_refusal_id,
            _R_WD,
            BLOCKED,
        ),
        (
            "markerless native refusal rejects a wrong-id retirement",
            5999999999,
            _R_MOVED_WD,
            BLOCKED,
        ),
    ]
    for name, target, withdrawal, want_rc in native_cases:
        result = run_command(
            [
                native_review,
                {
                    "id": command_withdrawal_id,
                    "body": _tag(
                        "hermit-121", f"{withdrawal}\nRETIRES #{target}"
                    ),
                    "user": {"login": "hermit-121"},
                    "_review_surface": "issue-comment",
                    "_current_review_actor_authorized": True,
                    "_current_review_actor_login": "hermit-121",
                    "_current_review_actor_permission": "write",
                    "created_at": "2026-09-04T00:00:02Z",
                    "updated_at": "2026-09-04T00:00:02Z",
                },
            ]
        )
        if result.returncode != want_rc:
            bad.append(
                f"command: {name} -- rc={result.returncode}, want {want_rc}; "
                f"stdout={result.stdout[:180]!r}; stderr={result.stderr[:100]!r}"
            )
        if no_verdict_in(result.stdout):
            bad.append(f"command: {name} -- no verdict reached stdout")

    missing_id = run_command([{"body": _R_REF}])
    if (
        missing_id.returncode != INDETERMINATE
        or "has no readable numeric id" not in missing_id.stdout
    ):
        bad.append(
            "command: a comment object without an id did not fail INDETERMINATE "
            f"instead of being assigned a position -- rc={missing_id.returncode}; "
            f"stdout={missing_id.stdout[:180]!r}"
        )

    for b in bad:
        print(f"changes-requested-gate self-test: FAIL -- {b}", file=sys.stderr)
    if bad:
        return 1
    print(f"changes-requested-gate self-test: OK -- {len(cases)} withdrawal-scoping "
          f"case(s) including the same-lane pair from hermit#2625, plus {len(render)} "
          f"answered-vs-outrun rendering case(s) and their metadata control, plus "
          f"the old-head unsigned-withdrawal control and two-direction precise-"
          f"retirement case from hermit#2670, plus "
          f"{len(state_cases)} state case(s) from hermit#2677 and their over-reach "
          f"controls, plus the two-direction guard that ANSWERED states the clearing "
          f"FORM without asserting the finding was addressed, plus "
          f"{len(retires)} exact RETIRES case(s) with their authorization and "
          f"naming controls, plus "
          f"{len(relane)} later lane-decision discharge case(s) from hermit#2686 "
          f"and hermit#2692, their cross-lane, metadata and ordering "
          f"controls and the three that must fail, plus {len(absence)} "
          f"absence-is-not-a-pass case(s) and the four-outcome stdout invariant "
          f"with its provenance stamp, plus {len(command_cases)} end-to-end command "
          f"case(s) that preserve comment ids through JSON ingestion and the "
          f"missing-id fail-closed control, plus {len(chronology_cases)} shared "
          f"chronology command case(s) and the incomplete body-string control, plus "
          f"{len(native_cases)} native-review "
          f"retirement case(s)")
    return 0


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    p.add_argument("--repo", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--pr", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--head")
    p.add_argument("--source", help="a checkout, so a main-equal (wiped) head can be detected")
    p.add_argument(
        "--from-json",
        help="offline: a JSON array of complete comment/review objects; "
             "body-string arrays are incomplete and refused",
    )
    p.add_argument(
        "--require-answered",
        action="store_true",
        help="compatibility spelling: old-head refusals are always required to be "
             "answered; omitting this option no longer permits an OUTRUN refusal",
    )
    p.add_argument("--self-test", action="store_true",
                   help="exercise the withdrawal-scoping cases and exit")
    if argv is None:
        argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if "--self-test" in argv:
        return self_test()
    args = p.parse_args(argv)
    grammar = _load_grammar()

    if args.from_json:
        head = args.head or ""
        try:
            with open(args.from_json, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                records = json.load(fh)
            bodies, ids = comment_bodies_and_ids(records)
            if ids is None:
                return emit(
                    INDETERMINATE,
                    args.pr,
                    head,
                    "body-string input is incomplete: stable record ids, authoritative "
                    "version times, review surfaces, and immutable GitHub actor "
                    "permission are required. Pass complete comment/review objects "
                    "to --from-json, then retry",
                )
            if records and isinstance(records[0], dict):
                try:
                    grammar.validate_review_authority_annotations(records)
                except grammar.ReviewRecordError as exc:
                    return emit(
                        INDETERMINATE,
                        args.pr,
                        head,
                        "review authority annotations are incomplete: "
                        f"{exc}. Supply the immutable GitHub actor and explicit "
                        "current permission annotation for every authority-bearing "
                        "record, then retry",
                    )
            native_ids = native_changes_requested_ids(records)
            event_records = records if records and isinstance(records[0], dict) else None
        except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError, ValueError) as exc:
            return emit(
                INDETERMINATE,
                args.pr,
                head,
                f"could not read comment records from {args.from_json}: {exc}. "
                "Pass a readable JSON file to --from-json, then retry",
            )
    else:
        # ⚠ ONE QUERY, BOTH FACTS. `ci-hub/landing/land-pr.sh` reads `state` beside
        # `headRefName` in a single `gh pr view` for exactly this reason: a per-head
        # EXTRA read is what trips the shared secondary rate limit. `state` is a
        # fixed enum with no spaces, so on the common path it costs a field rather
        # than a round trip. When `--head` was supplied this path previously made no
        # view call at all, so there it costs one -- accepted, because the three
        # paginated comment reads below dwarf it and a state-blind verdict was worse
        # than a fourth field.
        view = gh_json(["pr", "view", args.pr, "-R", args.repo, "--json", "headRefOid,state"])
        view = view if isinstance(view, dict) else {}
        head = args.head or view.get("headRefOid")

        # ⚠ STATE IS DECIDED BEFORE ANY MARKER IS READ, and that ordering is part of
        # the fix. A merged pull request needs no comment scan: skipping it here also
        # removes three paginated reads from every moot query.
        verdict = state_verdict(view.get("state"))
        if verdict is not None:
            rc, why = verdict
            return emit(rc, args.pr, head, why)

        if not head:
            return emit(INDETERMINATE, args.pr, None,
                        "could not resolve the head sha")
        # ⚠️ A WIPED BRANCH IS NOT A CLEAN HEAD, AND IT LAUNDERS EVERY REFUSAL.
        # Something on this box closes pull requests and RESETS BRANCHES to
        # main-equal on a ~10 minute cadence -- hermit#2448 four times. When that
        # happens the head sha MOVES, so every refusal naming the old sha stops
        # binding and this gate reports the most reassuring verdict it has:
        # "refusal marker(s) present but none names <head> -- superseded by a later
        # push, the correct clearing path". That sentence is true of an author
        # pushing a fix and equally true of a branch that was wiped out from under
        # the review, and the gate cannot tell them apart from comments alone.
        #
        # A main-equal head also merges as a NO-OP, so the pull request closes as
        # "merged" while its content never landed -- a landing verified before a
        # wipe looks identical to one that reverted itself.
        #
        # Cheap discriminator: if the head carries nothing over main, it is not a
        # head worth clearing. Requires --source; without it this cannot be checked
        # and is skipped, which is stated rather than silent.
        # Issue comments are where every marker observed to date lives; reviews and
        # review comments were both EMPTY on hermit#2176, checked 2026-08-25. Read
        # all three anyway -- a container this gate does not read is a way past it.
        records, records_error = live_review_records(args.repo, args.pr)
        if records_error is not None:
            return emit(
                INDETERMINATE,
                args.pr,
                head,
                f"could not read complete review records: {records_error}; a failed "
                "query is not an empty result",
            )
        try:
            bodies, ids = comment_bodies_and_ids(records)
            native_ids = native_changes_requested_ids(records)
            event_records = records
        except ValueError as exc:
            return emit(
                INDETERMINATE,
                args.pr,
                head,
                f"could not preserve the comment ids returned by GitHub: {exc}",
            )

    rc, why = evaluate(
        bodies,
        head,
        ids=ids,
        require_answered=args.require_answered,
        native_refusal_ids=native_ids,
        records=event_records,
    )

    # A main-equal head cannot CLEAR a pull request that carries refusal markers.
    # Only downgrades OK -> INDETERMINATE; a BLOCKED verdict is already correct and
    # is left alone.
    if rc == OK and args.source and markers(bodies) and head_is_main_equal(args.source, head):
        rc, why = (
            INDETERMINATE,
            f"the head {head[:12]} carries NOTHING over main, and this pull request has "
            f"refusal marker(s) naming other shas. 'Superseded by a later push' and "
            f"'the branch was wiped out from under the review' are the same sentence "
            f"from comments alone, and a main-equal head merges as a no-op -- the pull "
            f"request would close as merged with nothing landed. Refusing until the head "
            f"carries the change again.",
        )

    return emit(rc, args.pr, head, why)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    # ⚠️ AN UNEXPECTED CRASH IS INDETERMINATE, NEVER BLOCKED. Both are non-zero and
    # both stop the merge, so safety does not depend on telling them apart -- but a
    # crash reported as BLOCKED reads afterwards as "a reviewer refused this head",
    # which is a claim about the pull request that nobody made.
    try:
        raise SystemExit(main())
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except BaseException as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 -- deliberately total
        # ⚠ ON STDOUT LIKE EVERY OTHER VERDICT. A crash that printed only to stderr
        # left the same empty stdout as a gate that was never installed, so the two
        # -- "it ran and broke" and "it was not here" -- rendered identically to the
        # reader who most needed to tell them apart.
        print(f"{VERDICT_PREFIX} INDETERMINATE pr=? head=? gate={gate_provenance()} -- "
              f"the gate itself failed to run ({type(exc).__name__}: {exc}). Nothing was "
              f"checked; this is NOT a statement about the pull request.")
        raise SystemExit(INDETERMINATE)
